{
  "name": "stolen-okta-session",
  "description": "Example 1 (graph-analytics docs/graph-examples.html#ex1), graph part only. Alice's stolen Okta session reaches three crown jewels: the customer PII bucket through eng-oncall, OncallRole and DataAdminRole or BreakGlassRole; the warehouse through the prod/warehouse-admin secret or the etl-server-01 pivot to ETLRole; and Domain Admins through da-jlee's credentials left on that server. Backward, her Okta user owns the macOS account asmith, which logs on to the laptop ACME-MBP-0412, and resolves to her AD account by email. Not here, because they come from alerts and logs at query time: the two alerts, the session and its IPs, the other users and sessions behind those IPs, the 13:52 'assumed next' (a change after the batch, not generated) and the CloudTrail evidence line. etl-server-01 is two nodes, the EC2 instance and its Defend host, joined by 'same machine' (R9), because credentials are left on a host.",
